//! The substantial routines.
//!
//! 1345 of them, and they are the big ones: 24637 steps between them, an average of
//! eighteen each, the largest running to 60 steps and the deepest to 28 levels
//! of branching inside branching. They make 35568 calls. Read by the same walk as
//! the smaller tables and recorded in the same shape, so everything that can be
//! asked of those can be asked of these.
//!
//! Their size is what makes them worth having together. One rule in particular
//! only becomes visible across the whole set of tables, and this table is where
//! it is strongest: **the work of a routine sits at its top, and thins with
//! every level of branching.** A step at the outermost level here makes about
//! two and a fifth calls; one level in, about one and a fifth; and it keeps
//! falling, level after level, without once going back up. The same fall
//! appears in every other table of this kind, from a lower start each time the
//! routines are smaller. Branching narrows what is being decided; it does not
//! carry the work.
//!
//! 769 of them have a loop. 49 are constructors, and in every one of those the
//! run time's make-an-instance and its after-it-is-made sit in different steps
//! — the fifth separate population in which that has now held with no
//! exception at all.

// The addresses are written without separators so the port-mapping importer can
// match them against the recovered functions.
